From ba0e86edc273f7b2787cc29f5f2db6baff427e7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 00:22:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] winebare.drv: the screen and controls of bare_app

A graphics driver for Wine run by bare_app_wine, a game of bare_app on
handhelds without X11 or Wayland: each Wine process connects to the
socket in WINEBARE_SOCKET and hands over the pixels of its top-level
windows (GDI surfaces, and OpenGL drawn into EGL pbuffers on Mesa's
surfaceless platform) in shared memory. The host composes and scales
them to bare_app's screen and sends mouse and keyboard input made from
the gamepad. One monitor whose size is the display mode; any listed mode
is set at once. explorer tries it first.

A GL swap does not wait for the GPU: the back buffer is blitted, upside
down, into a texture and fenced, and a thread with a context of its own
sharing the textures reads it into shared memory once the fence is
signalled. A synchronous glReadPixels on each swap kept the CPU idle
while the GPU drew (on panfrost glReadPixels into a pixel buffer object
is synchronous too: it has no blit-based transfers). WINEBARE_SYNC_READ=1
goes back to the synchronous read back, WINEBARE_FPS=1 prints frames a
second and the frame gaps over a quarter of a second on stderr.
